     return routes\n    \n    # Initialization: each customer as a separate route\n    routes = [[0, c, 0] for c in customers]\n    \n    # Merge until we have exactly truck_count routes\n    while len(routes) > truck_count:\n        best_saving = -1e9\n        best_pair = None\n        best_order = 0\n        for i in range(len(routes)):\n            for j in range(i+1, len(routes)):\n                r_i = routes[i]\n                r_j = routes[j]\n                last_i = r_i[-2]\n                first_i = r_i[1]\n                last_j = r_j[-2]\n                first_j = r_j[1]\n                s1 = distance_matrix[0][last_i] + distance_matrix[0][first_j] - distance_matrix[last_i][first_j]\n                s2 = distance_matrix[0][last_j] + distance_matrix[0][first_i] - distance_matrix[last_j][first_i]\n                if s1 > best_saving:\n                    best_saving = s1\n                    best_pair = (i, j)\n                    best_order = 0\n                if s2 > best_saving:\n                    best_saving = s2\n                    best_pair = (i, j)\n                    best_order = 1\n        if best_pair is None:\n            break\n        i, j = best_pair\n        if best_order == 0:\n            new_route = routes[i][:-1] + routes[j][1:]\n        else:\n            new_route = routes[j][:-1] + routes[i][1:]\n        if i < j:\n            del routes[j]\n            del routes[i]\n        else:\n            del routes[i]\n            del routes[j]\n        routes.append(new_route)\n    \n    # Report initial construction\n    report_best_vrp(routes)\n    \n    # Compute initial distances\n    dists = [route_distance(r, distance_matrix) for r in routes]\n    best_max = max(dists)\n    best_routes = [list(r) for r in routes]\n    \n    # Local search: focus on longest route\n    max_iter = 2 * n\n    for _ in range(max_iter):\n        current_max = max(dists)\n        # Find longest route (first if tie)\n        longest_idx = max(range(len(dists)), key=lambda i: dists[i])\n        longest_route = routes[longest_idx]\n        best_improvement = 0\n        best_move = None\n        \n        # For each customer in the longest route (skip depot nodes)\n        for pos_in_longest, cust in enumerate(longest_route):\n            if cust == 0:\n                continue\n            # Compute removal cost from longest route\n            prev_node = longest_route[pos_in_longest-1]\n            next_node = longest_route[pos_in_longest+1]\n            removal_saving = distance_matrix[prev_node, cust] + distance_matrix[cust, next_node] - distance_matrix[prev_node, next_node]\n            new_long_len = dists[longest_idx] - removal_saving\n            \n            # Try inserting into other routes\n            for r_idx, route in enumerate(routes):\n                if r_idx == longest_idx:\n                    continue\n                for pos in range(1, len(route)):  # insertion between route[pos-1] and route[pos]\n                    # Avoid inserting into position that would duplicate customer\n                    if route[pos] == cust or route[pos-1] == cust:\n                        continue\n                    insertion_cost = distance_matrix[route[pos-1], cust] + distance_matrix[cust, route[pos]] - distance_matrix[route[pos-1], route[pos]]\n                    new_target_len = dists[r_idx] + insertion_cost\n                    # Compute new max\n                    new_max = max(new_long_len, new_target_len)\n                    # Consider other routes' distances unchanged\n                    if new_max < current_max - 1e-9:\n                        improvement = current_max - new_max\n                        if improvement > best_improvement:\n                            best_improvement = improvement\n                            best_move = (longest_idx, pos_in_longest, r_idx, pos, cust)\n        \n        if best_move is not None:\n            longest_idx, pos_in_longest, r_idx, pos, cust = best_move\n            # Apply move\n            routes[longest_idx].pop(pos_in_longest)\n            routes[r_idx].insert(pos, cust)\n            # Update distances\n            dists[longest_idx] = route_distance(routes[longest_idx], distance_matrix)\n            dists[r_idx] = route_distance(routes[r_idx], distance_matrix)\n            new_max = max(dists)\n            if new_max < best_max - 1e-9:\n                best_max = new_max\n                best_routes = [list(r) for r in routes]\n                report_best_vrp(best_routes)\n        else:\n            break\n    \n    return best_routes\n```\n\nImplement exactly this interface:\n\ndef solve_vrp(\n    distance_matrix: np.ndarray,  # (n, n), depot is node 0\n    truck_count: int,\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n\nRules:\n- VRP is a static multi-truck routing problem; the objective is to minimize the maximum route distance across trucks.\n- Return exactly truck_count routes.\n- Each route must start at depot node 0 and end at depot node 0.\n- Every customer node 1..n-1 must appear exactly once across all routes.\n- An unused truck route must be [0, 0].\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates, instance size, truck count, or dataset details.\n- Keep all search and improvement loops finite and bounded by instance size; never use open-ended loops.\n- Call report_best_vrp(routes) whenever you find a better complete feasible route set, especially before expensive improvement loops.\n- Do not read/write files, use network, spawn subprocesses, or call external solvers.\n- Allowed imports only: numpy, math, random, heapq, itertools, collections, time.\n- No module-level mutable globals; the solver may be called many times across instances.\n\nInternal check before final JSON: correct solve_vrp signature, exactly truck_count routes,\nevery route starts and ends at 0, every customer appears exactly once, empty trucks use [0, 0],\nfinite instance-size-bounded loops, deterministic tie handling, report_best_vrp used for\ninc